## 3. Easy API conventions

### Where things go

```
lib/easy-api/
  server.ts        ← base service, exports `easyApi` singleton + types
  types.ts         ← (when added) shared envelope types from Easy API spec
  projects.ts      ← domain methods: getProject, listProjects, createProject…
  transactions.ts  ← domain methods
  users.ts
  documents.ts
  waitlist.ts
```

Domain modules import `easyApi` and export typed functions — they do **not** create new service instances. One module per Firestore collection mirrors the action-file split, so the call sites change minimally.

### Domain method shape

```ts
// lib/easy-api/projects.ts
import { easyApi, type EasyApiResponse } from './server';

export type ProjectDto = Project & { id: string };

export const getProject = (id: string) =>
  easyApi.get<ProjectDto, ApiErrorBody>(`/projects/${id}`);

export const listProjects = (query?: { status?: ProjectStatus; type?: ProjectType }) =>
  easyApi.get<ProjectDto[], ApiErrorBody>('/projects', { query });

export const createProject = (body: Omit<Project, 'id' | 'createdAt'>) =>
  easyApi.post<ProjectDto, ApiErrorBody>('/projects', { body });
```

Return the `EasyApiResponse<TData, TError>` discriminated union directly — do **not** unwrap `.data` inside the domain layer. Callers (route handlers) decide how to surface the error.

### Validation

Use **zod parsing on the boundary** when the response shape is non-trivial or untrusted; cast for thin pass-throughs. Define schemas in the same domain file:

```ts
const projectDtoSchema = z.object({ /* mirror Project */ });
const parsed = await getProject(id);
if (parsed.ok) projectDtoSchema.parse(parsed.data);
```

Don't add zod schemas preemptively — wait for the actual Easy API response shape.

